# Verified against the running device with `ls -Z`: every file directly under
# soc:lenovo_kb/ carries sysfs_kb, except wakeup/, which the kernel's own
# wakeup-source labelling claims as sysfs_wakeup.
#
# ⚠️ This used to end "The order matters — the more specific wakeup entry has to
# come after the directory entry." It does not. Source order in this file has no
# effect on the result: checkpolicy re-sorts genfscon by DECREASING name length
# when emitting CIL, and secilc sorts again
# (external/selinux/libsepol/cil/src/cil_post.c:303-315). Measured on the built
# policy — vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil has .../soc:lenovo_kb/wakeup at
# line 364 and .../soc:lenovo_kb at line 392, i.e. this file's order INVERTED.
# The policy also contains a `genfscon sysfs "/"` entry and 592 nested pairs, so
# specificity is resolved by the compiler and the kernel, not here.
#
# The two lines below are still both required and still correct; only the stated
# reason was wrong. Keeping them adjacent is readability, not semantics.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:lenovo_kb            u:object_r:sysfs_kb:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:lenovo_kb/wakeup      u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0

# The other two wakeup sources on this device that do NOT get sysfs_wakeup from
# the kernel, and that stock labels here. Both were missing, and the first one
# is an every-boot denial on this build (session 24):
#
#   avc: denied { read open } comm="android.system."
#        path=".../spi4/spi4.0/wakeup/wakeup20/name"
#        scontext=u:r:system_suspend:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# ⚠️ This is a LABEL defect, not a rule defect, which is why a rule-level diff
# against stock does not find it: `allow system_suspend sysfs_wakeup:file
# { read open getattr }` is present in BOTH policies. Stock simply never hits
# the `sysfs` type, because its genfs labels these two nodes and ours did not.
#
# Enumerated closed rather than guessed -- every wakeup source on the device was
# resolved to its live label, and these are the only two of 40-odd that come
# back plain `sysfs`:
#
#   for w in /sys/class/wakeup/*; do r=$(readlink -f $w); ls -Zd $r; done
#     u:object_r:sysfs:s0  .../a90000.spi/spi_master/spi4/spi4.0/wakeup/wakeup20
#     u:object_r:sysfs:s0  .../soc:qcom,pmic_glink_log:bm_adsp_ulog/wakeup/wakeup43
#
# spi4.0 is the Novatek touch controller (`cat .../spi4.0/modalias` ->
# spi:NVT-ts-spi), so the cost of leaving it unlabelled is that touch-wake loses
# its attribution in `dumpsys suspend_control` and in battery wakeup blame.
#
# Both paths are copied verbatim from stock's compiled policy
# (work/unpacked/parts/vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil:44 and :53), so
# they are Lenovo's own strings for Lenovo's own board, not a reconstruction.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a90000.spi/spi_master/spi4/spi4.0/wakeup                    u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:qcom,pmic_glink_log/soc:qcom,pmic_glink_log:bm_adsp_ulog/wakeup                     u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0

# The stylus and folio knobs. Stock leaves these as plain "proc" and lets its
# touchscreen HAL write any proc file; labelling them lets that HAL be granted
# these four alone (see hal_lenovo.te).
genfscon proc /pen_type                                        u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /pen_attached                                    u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /pen_wakeup_mode                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /folio_case_mode                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0

# The other /proc nodes the touchscreen HAL opens. See file.te.
#
# ⚠️ The authority for this list is the HAL BINARY, not the stock .rc:
#
#   strings vendor/bin/hw/vendor.lenovo.hardware.touchscreen-service \
#     | grep '^/proc/'          -> 13 paths
#
# The stock .rc is written for a whole SKU line and chowns paths that do not
# exist on this unit. Taking the list from it put /proc/gesture_control here --
# a node that does not exist (verified on the device: `ls -Z` says MISSING) --
# while leaving /proc/gesture_mode and /proc/support_pen, which the HAL really
# does open, at plain u:object_r:proc:s0 and therefore denied under enforcing.
#
# file.te already recorded that gesture_control "is not created on this unit";
# what was missed is that the real node is gesture_mode, under a different name.
#
# gesture_mode and support_pen are root:root 0666 on stock rather than
# system:system like the other eleven, because the stock .rc never chowns them.
# Mode 0666 means DAC is not the gate here -- SELinux is.
genfscon proc /panel_direction                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /game_mode                                       u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /game_edge                                       u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /edge_grid_zone                                  u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /report_threshold                                u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /HighReportRate                                  u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /haptics                                         u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /gesture_mode                                    u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /support_pen                                     u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0

# /proc/nvt_fw_version is deliberately NOT labelled here, even though the stock
# vendor_sepolicy.cil labels it (vendor_proc_tpver) and grants platform_app and
# system_app read access. Recording the decision because a diff against stock
# will keep surfacing it as "the one device-specific label we are missing".
#
# By this file's own rule -- the authority is the consumer, not the stock policy
# -- it has no consumer here. A binary-safe search finds zero references to
# nvt_fw_version in anything this tree installs, and, notably, zero in the
# factory's vendor and system *binaries* too. Its reader is a Lenovo
# factory-test app, i.e. dex on the ZUI product partition, which PixelOS
# replaces wholesale.
#
# Adding it would also mean vendor policy granting two coredomains
# (platform_app, system_app) access to a vendor-labelled type, which is the
# direction Treble's neverallows exist to discourage. If a consumer ever ships,
# add the label and the grant together with it.

# Awinic smart-amplifier nodes (aw882xx_chipid and the driver's other per-device
# attributes). Four AW88461 on QUP i2c bus 5 at 0x34, 0x35, 0x36 and 0x37.
# Path read off the running device rather than guessed:
#   /sys/devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0034/aw882xx_chipid
# The four clients are named one by one rather than labelling the bus: i2c-5
# also carries 5-003e (AW37504 display bias) and 5-0064 (RTC6226 FM tuner), and
# genfscon prefix-matches, so a bus-level entry would hand the audio HAL read
# access to two unrelated devices.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0034 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0035 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0036 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0037 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0

# Lenovo/Tinno per-component hardware inventory. hal_sensors_default writes its
# own entry here at probe; see sepolicy/vendor/measured.te for the readout and
# for why this gets a type instead of the sysfs blanket stock uses.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/product-device-info                          u:object_r:sysfs_product_device_info:s0

# /sys/class/qcom-battery — qti_battery_charger.ko's class attributes, of which
# charge_control_en is the gate that makes the two
# power_supply/battery/charge_control_*_threshold nodes writable at all. Stock
# leaves the whole class as plain sysfs; giving it a type lets init.malbec.rc
# open the gate at boot without vendor_init needing write access to all of
# sysfs. The threshold nodes themselves already carry
# vendor_sysfs_battery_supply from the QTI policy.
genfscon sysfs /class/qcom-battery                                           u:object_r:sysfs_qcom_battery:s0

# LiveDisplay Outdoor mode (high brightness mode), and the recovery backlight.
#
# device.mk installs vendor.lineage.livedisplay-service.sysfs with
# se_path=/sys/class/backlight/panel0-backlight/hbm. That path is a symlink;
# genfscon matches the REAL device path, which on this unit is
#   /sys/devices/platform/soc/ae00000.qcom,mdss_mdp/backlight/panel0-backlight
# (readlink -f on the running device).
#
# ⚠️ TWO entries, and the split is load-bearing. Session 21 first wrote ONE line
# labelling the whole directory sysfs_livedisplay_tuneable, and that was wrong
# in both directions at once:
#
#   · The HAL could not have used it. Its only grant is
#     device/lineage/sepolicy/common/vendor/hal_lineage_livedisplay_sysfs.te:10,
#     `allow ... sysfs_livedisplay_tuneable:file rw_file_perms;` — there is no
#     :dir rule on that type anywhere, and the blanket
#     `allow domain sysfs:dir search` (system/sepolicy/private/domain.te:272)
#     covers PLAIN sysfs only. Upstream gets away with labelling only files
#     because it leaves their parent directory plain. Relabel the parent and the
#     HAL loses `search` on the directory it has to traverse.
#
#   · It took the label recovery needs. init.recovery.qcom.rc:25 writes
#     panel0-backlight/brightness from the `init` domain (recovery mounts no
#     /vendor, so there is no vendor_init subcontext), and the only rule that
#     permits it is device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/
#     init.te:90-95, `recovery_only(allow init vendor_sysfs_graphics:file
#     w_file_perms; allow init vendor_sysfs_graphics:dir search;)`. Plain sysfs
#     cannot be granted instead — private/init.te:862 neverallows
#     `init sysfs:file { open write }`.
#
# So: the directory and brightness get vendor_sysfs_graphics, and only the one
# tuneable gets the LiveDisplay type. genfscon is longest-prefix, which is the
# same mechanism the soc:lenovo_kb / soc:lenovo_kb/wakeup pair above relies on.
#
# The HAL's missing `search` on the new parent type is granted in
# sepolicy/vendor/hal_lineage_livedisplay.te — one rule, and it is required.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ae00000.qcom,mdss_mdp/backlight/panel0-backlight     u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_graphics:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ae00000.qcom,mdss_mdp/backlight/panel0-backlight/hbm u:object_r:sysfs_livedisplay_tuneable:s0

# ★ The two UFS LUNs QTI's own genfs rules mislabel on this unit, because it
# assumes an enumeration order this device does not have.
#
# device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/…/genfs_contexts labels all eight LUNs on the
# assumption that LUN index N is /sys/block/sd<N-th letter>:
#
#     …/0:0:0:3/block/sdd/queue/rq_affinity   u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
#     …/0:0:0:4/block/sde/queue/rq_affinity   u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
#
# ⚠️⚠️ THE LETTER ASSIGNMENT RACES PER BOOT. DO NOT DELETE THE FOUR RULES BELOW
# AS REDUNDANT — that is the exact mistake this paragraph now exists to prevent.
#
# Session 34 measured, with `readlink -f`:
#
#     0:0:0:3 -> sde      (65,536 sectors, the RPMB LUN)
#     0:0:0:4 -> sdd      (8,388,608 sectors, a boot LUN)
#
# and recorded it as a property of this unit ("on this tablet those two are
# SWAPPED"). ★ SESSION 36 MEASURED THE OPPOSITE ON THE SAME TABLET:
#
#     0:0:0:3 -> sdd      (65,536 sectors)
#     0:0:0:4 -> sde      (8,388,608 sectors)
#
# The SIZES still identify the LUNs the same way; only the sd* letters flipped.
# So this is not a device property at all — the letters are handed out in
# whatever order the LUNs finish probing, and both orders really happen.
#
# ✔ NOTHING IS WRONG AND NOTHING NEEDS CHANGING, and that is precisely why the
# temptation to "clean up" is dangerous. The four rules below are ADDITIVE:
# QTI's own two rules (device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/parrot/
# genfs_contexts) cover the natural order, ours cover the swapped one, and the
# union covers both. On the boot where the letters land the other way, ours
# name paths that do not exist and QTI's do the labelling — which is exactly
# what was observed in session 36: the live nodes were correctly labelled
# `vendor_sysfs_sd` while our four paths were absent.
#
# ⇒ Deleting these because "the paths don't exist" would break `rq_affinity`
# labelling on roughly half of all boots, and the failure would be
# intermittent — the worst possible shape.
#
# genfscon matches the whole path, so on the boot session 34 measured, QTI's two
# rules matched nothing and exactly those two nodes fell through to plain
# `sysfs`. The other six LUNs enumerate in the assumed order either way.
#
# ★ HOW THIS WAS FOUND, because the method is the point. It was invisible until
# session 34 deleted `allow vendor_qti_init_shell sysfs:file rw_file_perms` —
# the blanket grant that let the write succeed on ANY plain-sysfs file and so
# hid the mislabelling. The MALBEC_NO_DONTAUDIT diagnostic boot then produced:
#
#     avc: denied { write } for comm="sh" name="rq_affinity" dev="sysfs"
#          ino=92075 scontext=u:r:vendor_qti_init_shell:s0
#          tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# and ino 92075 resolved to /sys/block/sde/queue/rq_affinity. The writer is
# /vendor/bin/init.kernel.post_boot-tuna.sh:81-82, `for sd in
# /sys/block/sd*/queue/rq_affinity`, "set rq_affinity to 0 on ufs devices".
# Measured after the removal: six LUNs read 0 and sdd/sde read 1.
#
# ⚠️ Stock has the identical mislabelling and does not notice, because it ships
# the blanket rule. Two labels are strictly better than one blanket grant: this
# fixes the two nodes that were wrong instead of permitting every plain-sysfs
# file on the device.
#
# The functional stake is small and is stated rather than oversold: rq_affinity
# is block-layer completion affinity on a 64 KB RPMB LUN and an 8 MB boot LUN,
# neither of which carries meaningful I/O. read_ahead_kb is labelled with it for
# the same reason QTI labels the pair together — nothing writes it on these two
# today, and leaving one half of a mislabelled pair fixed is how the next reader
# gets confused.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/1d84000.ufshc/host0/target0:0:0/0:0:0:3/block/sde/queue/rq_affinity    u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/1d84000.ufshc/host0/target0:0:0/0:0:0:3/block/sde/queue/read_ahead_kb   u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/1d84000.ufshc/host0/target0:0:0/0:0:0:4/block/sdd/queue/rq_affinity    u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/1d84000.ufshc/host0/target0:0:0/0:0:0:4/block/sdd/queue/read_ahead_kb   u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sd:s0
